{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Report\n\n`rebar transition <id> in_progress closed` can print an error to stderr **with exit code 0**,\n~60s AFTER the close is already durably committed, and silently downgrade the ticket to\nclosed-WITHOUT-signature. Observed in the field: an agent reported its final `rebar\ntransition` failed with a lock error; re-reading showed the transition had LANDED.\n\n## Premise verified in current `origin/main` (cc69cc1179)\n\n`src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py`, inside `close_ticket` (line **263**):\n\n* The STATUS event is committed and the lock released BEFORE the signing tail runs.\n* Line **433** calls `sign_completion_verdict(...)`, whose append goes through\n  `write_and_push` -> `event_append` -> a fresh 60s `write_lock` acquisition.\n* Line **434** catches `_signing.SigningError` and lines **439-445** write\n  `Warning: closed <id> WITHOUT a completion signature — <exc.message> ...` to stderr and\n  fall through. `close_ticket` then returns its normal success payload (lines **492-498**:\n  `ticket_id`, `from`, `to`, `newly_unblocked`, `noop`) and the command exits **0**.\n* The returned payload carries **no field at all** about the signature outcome, so no\n  library, MCP or CLI consumer can detect the loss programmatically.\n* The sibling drift refusal (lines **421-429**) lands unsigned the same way, likewise with\n  no payload field.\n\nThe `except` comment (lines **435-438**) justifies the exit-0 degrade for a *missing\nssh-keygen* — a permanent, operator-fixable condition. That rationale does not transfer to a\n**transient lock timeout**, which is what the field incident hit, and the raw appended\n`exc.message` (\"flock: could not acquire lock after 60s\") reads as the CLOSE having failed\nwhen the close in fact succeeded.\n\nNote: bug royal-weariless-zebrafish (merged cc69cc1179) added bounded retry at the\n`event_append` write lock, making the signature append far likelier to survive contention.\nThat MITIGATES frequency; it does not fix the reporting shape.\n\n## Direction\n\nMake the loss **machine-readable, honestly worded, and durably detectable** — without\nchanging the process exit code.\n\n1. **Payload marker.** `close_ticket` returns an additional\n   `completion_signature: {\"signed\": bool, \"cause\": str, \"error\": str}` on every close-path\n   outcome: signed, `sign_failed` (the line-434 catch), `material_drifted` (line 421), and\n   the `--force` bypass. This mirrors the machine-branchable\n   `PlanReviewAttestation.as_dict()` idiom already used for exactly this problem in\n   `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/resign.py:457`, so a caller branches on `cause` without\n   parsing English.\n2. **Honest message.** The stderr text leads with the fact that the close **LANDED** and only\n   the signature did not, then names the recovery — instead of appending a bare lock error\n   that describes the succeeded operation as failed.\n3. **Durable detection.** Best-effort audit comment on the ticket recording\n   closed-without-signature and its cause, mirroring the existing FORCE_CLOSE audit comment\n   the same function already writes (line **473** region). Best-effort and swallowed on\n   failure, exactly like that sibling — it must never wedge a close that already committed.\n\n### Why NOT a nonzero exit code (deliberate, recorded)\n\nThe ticket's report offers \"a nonzero exit **or at minimum** a machine-readable failure\nmarker\". The exit path is rejected here for two concrete reasons:\n\n* `docs/exit-codes.md` declares the contract **frozen** as of the 2026-06-09 breaking-change\n  window; it is pinned by `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py`, and the table at\n  `docs/exit-codes.md:158` fixes `transition` at 0 success / 1 error / 10 mismatch. A NEW\n  code is additive-only in a future window — a release decision, not an implementation one.\n* Reusing exit **1** would be actively harmful: agents read 1 as \"the transition failed\" and\n  re-run it, which then exits **10** (status mismatch, already closed). That trades a silent\n  loss for a louder wrong answer.\n\nThe marker route satisfies the reporting need within the frozen contract. Raising a new exit\ncode is left to a contract window and is explicitly out of scope here.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A close whose completion-signature append raises `SigningError` returns a payload with\n      `completion_signature.signed == False` and a `cause` naming the failure — verified by a\n      test that forces the append to fail and asserts the payload\n- [ ] A successful close returns `completion_signature.signed == True` — verified by a test\n- [ ] The stderr text on that failure states the close LANDED and only the signature did not,\n      and does not present the lock error as the close failing — verified by a test asserting\n      the message text\n- [ ] The closed-without-signature state is detectable by a later reader via a durable record\n      on the ticket — verified by a test reading the ticket after such a close\n- [ ] The material-drift refusal path also reports `completion_signature.signed == False`\n      with a distinct `cause` — verified by a test\n- [ ] Exit code is unchanged (0) on all of the above — verified by a test, pinning the\n      deliberate decision above"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786558335255619001,"uuid":"e29491b4-85cd-4abc-8efb-0d69141e233e"}